+/*@
+   EPSKrylovSchurSetCheckInterval - Sets the number of Arnoldi steps between the convergence checks
+   performed within a cycle of the Krylov-Schur method.
+
+   Logically Collective
+
+   Input Parameters:
++  eps   - the linear eigensolver context
+-  check - the number of steps between checks (0 to check only at the end of each cycle)
+
+   Options Database Key:
+.  -eps_krylovschur_check_interval <check> - sets the check interval
+
+   Notes:
+   By default, the Krylov-Schur method tests convergence once per cycle (restart), after the
+   factorization has been extended to the full basis size, so a solve that converges in its first
+   cycle always costs ncv applications of the operator. With a positive interval, the projected
+   problem is also solved and the convergence and stopping tests applied every check steps once
+   the factorization has nev columns, and the cycle ends as soon as the stopping test is satisfied
+   and each wanted Ritz value is within the tolerance (relative) of a Ritz value of the previous
+   check, since the residual estimates can be far below the error of Ritz values which have not
+   settled. A check does not end the cycle either while two of the converged Ritz values are within a
+   relative distance of 1e-6 of each other (back-transformed): the other copies of a multiple
+   eigenvalue enter a Krylov subspace generated from one start vector only through rounding errors,
+   after more steps, so problems with multiple eigenvalues (as with symmetries) keep the end-of-cycle
+   test. Nor does a check end the first cycle, which extends the factorization to the full basis size
+   as without checks: the first multiple eigenvalue of a problem can have a single converged copy in
+   it, with no other pair of converged Ritz values to keep the cycle running. A check that is not
+   satisfied leaves the iteration unchanged. Each check costs a dense
+   eigensolve of the projected problem, which is negligible when the operator applications are
+   expensive, as in shift-and-invert with iterative linear solves.
+
+   The checks apply to the Arnoldi variant with the standard extraction, without arbitrary
+   selection or threshold stopping; other cases ignore this setting.
+
+   Level: advanced
+
+.seealso: [](ch:eps), `EPSKRYLOVSCHUR`, `EPSKrylovSchurGetCheckInterval()`, `EPSSetDimensions()`
+@*/
+PetscErrorCode EPSKrylovSchurSetCheckInterval(EPS eps,PetscInt check)
+{
+  PetscFunctionBegin;
+  PetscValidHeaderSpecific(eps,EPS_CLASSID,1);
+  PetscValidLogicalCollectiveInt(eps,check,2);
+  PetscTryMethod(eps,"EPSKrylovSchurSetCheckInterval_C",(EPS,PetscInt),(eps,check));
+  PetscFunctionReturn(PETSC_SUCCESS);
+}
+
+static PetscErrorCode EPSKrylovSchurGetCheckInterval_KrylovSchur(EPS eps,PetscInt *check)
+{
+  EPS_KRYLOVSCHUR *ctx = (EPS_KRYLOVSCHUR*)eps->data;
+
+  PetscFunctionBegin;
+  *check = ctx->check;
+  PetscFunctionReturn(PETSC_SUCCESS);
+}
+
+/*@
+   EPSKrylovSchurGetCheckInterval - Gets the number of Arnoldi steps between the convergence checks
+   performed within a cycle of the Krylov-Schur method.
+
+   Not Collective
+
+   Input Parameter:
+.  eps - the linear eigensolver context
+
+   Output Parameter:
+.  check - the check interval
+
+   Level: advanced
+
+.seealso: [](ch:eps), `EPSKRYLOVSCHUR`, `EPSKrylovSchurSetCheckInterval()`
+@*/
+PetscErrorCode EPSKrylovSchurGetCheckInterval(EPS eps,PetscInt *check)
+{
+  PetscFunctionBegin;
+  PetscValidHeaderSpecific(eps,EPS_CLASSID,1);
+  PetscAssertPointer(check,2);
+  PetscUseMethod(eps,"EPSKrylovSchurGetCheckInterval_C",(EPS,PetscInt*),(eps,check));
+  PetscFunctionReturn(PETSC_SUCCESS);
+}
